  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1042 PM MDT Sat Sep 19 2026\n\nIsolated to scattered showers and thunderstorms will continue\nlate this evening, becoming more isolated in nature overnight\nmainly across the north as the upper low that was responsible\nfor our active weather period pushes eastward across the north.\nOverall, storm intensity appears on the downward trend as drier\nair continues to advect into the region behind this low and\nacross the Four Corners.\n\nDRYING OUT SUNDAY AND MONDAY:\n\nSunday and Monday will see much drier conditions in addition to\ncooler more seasonable temperatures as the upper low exits east\nonto the Plains and high pressure ridge builds out west over the\nGreat Basin. Enough moisture may still linger for some isolated\nshowers and storms over the higher terrain, but those chances\nare very low. Overall, Sunday and Monday should provide a brief\nbreak from the stormy pattern with cooler and pleasant\nconditions.\n\nPRECIPITATION CHANCES RETURN TUESDAY ONWARD:\n\nMeanwhile, all eyes will be on our next weather maker, an upper\nlow that will carve out over the Desert Southwest before lifting\nacross the Great Basin as an open wave trough Tuesday. This will\nshift the flow back to a southwest direction and allow moisture\nto move back into the region. High pressure sets up over Texas,\nwhich works in concert with the Great Basin trough to draw up\nmoisture into the Four Corners and across western Colorado. So\nit does appear like storm chances and coverage will increase\nTuesday and Wednesday, possibly lingering through late week,\nwith scattered coverage. We will be in the right entrance region\nof the jet, which helps provide dynamic support. The wave will\nalso provide shear so some of the storms could be strong to \nsevere. Also, freezing levels may come down enough to coat the \nhighest peaks with snow. The uncertainty is will the wave push \nthe moisture to our south or will it linger in portions of the \narea. If it does get pushed southward how quick will it return.\nConfidence in the forecast mid to late week is low attm.",
